"The Throne" in the Context of Iconic Chairs in Art and Design History
A Comparison between the "Post-Perception" Icon and 20th-Century Masterpieces: Positioning and Contribution
Ibrahim Shalabi
Conceptual Artist and Researcher — Founder of the “Post-Perception” Movement
________________________________________
I. Early Modernist Chairs
1. Red Blue Chair (1917) – Gerrit Rietveld (De Stijl)
•	Description: A wooden chair painted in primary colors (red, blue, yellow) and black, characterized by rigid geometric lines and perpendicular planes without curves.
•	The Concept: Reducing objects to their simplest elements (vertical and horizontal lines); the influence of Mondrian.
•	The Function: A chair for sitting (theoretically, though practically uncomfortable).
•	When Inverted: It loses its function entirely; it becomes impossible to sit on.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction: * Rietveld seeks geometric beauty; The Throne seeks the continuity of meaning.
o	Rietveld’s chair cannot withstand inversion; The Throne is designed specifically to embrace it.
2. Wassily Chair (1925–1926) – Marcel Breuer (Bauhaus)
•	Description: The first chair in history made of chrome-plated tubular steel with canvas straps.
•	The Concept: "Form follows function"; the union of art and industry.
•	The Function: A comfortable, mass-producible chair.
•	When Inverted: It loses its function; the tubes cannot bear weight in an inverted position.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction:
o	Breuer seeks utility and industry; The Throne seeks meaning and discovery.
o	Breuer’s chair is industrial and replicable; The Throne is unique, sculptural, and experimental.
________________________________________
II. Conceptual Art and Deconstruction Chairs
3. One and Three Chairs (1965) – Joseph Kosuth (Conceptual Art)
•	Description: A physical chair, a photograph of that chair, and a dictionary definition of the word "chair."
•	The Concept: The idea is more important than the physical object; deconstructing the relationship between the thing, its image, and its definition.
•	The Function: The physical chair is sit-able, but the artistic context prohibits its use.
•	When Inverted: It loses its function within the artistic context.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction:
o	Kosuth asks: "What is a chair?" (Philosophy of Language).
o	The Throne asks: "Is what we see sufficient for us to know?" (Philosophy of Perception).
o	Kosuth requires reading; The Throne requires a corporeal experience.
________________________________________
III. Postmodern Chairs
4. Untitled (Chair) (1980s) – Robert Gober (Postmodernism)
•	Description: A distorted or inverted wooden chair, sometimes embedded with nails or body parts (a leg).
•	The Concept: Distorting the familiar; referencing the missing body, violence, or illness.
•	The Function: Unfit for sitting due to distortion and nails.
•	When Inverted: It remains unfit for use; its dysfunction is its essence.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction:
o	Gober asks: "What happens when the familiar breaks?"
o	The Throne asks: "What happens when the familiar continues to function despite the breakage of its form?"
o	Gober’s chair is somber and painful; The Throne is calm and contemplative.
________________________________________
IV. Organic and Ergonomic Design
5. Paimio Chair (1932) – Alvar Aalto (Organic Modernism)
•	Description: Curved plywood chair, organically shaped, designed specifically for tuberculosis patients to aid breathing.
•	The Concept: Design serving the weary body; form follows human (rather than industrial) function.
•	When Inverted: Loses its function entirely.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction: Aalto seeks physical comfort; The Throne seeks a test of consciousness.
6. Eames Lounge Chair (1956) – Charles & Ray Eames (Mid-Century Modern)
•	Description: Plywood and leather; luxury, comfort, and a symbol of high taste.
•	The Concept: Good design for the masses (though it became an elite icon).
•	When Inverted: Loses its function.
•	The Post-Perception Distinction: Eames seeks luxury and relaxation; The Throne seeks awareness and discovery.
