V. Comparative Synthesis
1. The Inversion Paradox
In history, almost every iconic chair loses its function when inverted. "The Throne" is unique; it is engineered to retain its utility in two opposing positions. This is not a flaw in the others, but the specific "haecceity" (essence) of The Throne.
2. Relationship with the Recipient
•	Rietveld: Spectator contemplating geometric beauty.
•	Breuer: User testing industrial comfort.
•	Kosuth: Reader/Analyst of linguistic concepts.
•	Gober: Observer of anxiety and trauma.
•	The Throne: Experimentalist, physical participant, and discoverer.
•	Conclusion: The Throne does not settle for being watched or thought about. It demands direct physical engagement to discover what the eye fails to see.
3. The Central Inquiry
•	Modernism: Can art be reduced to lines/colors? Can design serve the modern man?
•	Conceptualism: What is the relationship between the object and its definition?
•	Post-Perception: Is what we see enough to define the object? Or does perception require the involvement of the entire body?
________________________________________
Final Conclusion: Where does "The Throne" Stand?
•	Historically: It arrives after a century of chairs that lost their function to art (Duchamp, Kosuth, Gober) to announce that function can survive deconstruction.
•	Functionally: It is the only chair that maintains its utility in two diametrically opposed states.
•	Philosophically: It defines a chair not as a form, an idea, or a utility alone, but as a physical experience.
The Throne is a chair that invites you to sit on it while it is upside down, only for you to discover that what you deemed "impossible" is entirely "possible." This is the core of Post-Perception.

